Railway's HTTP log shows the edge reached the container both times and waited: `GET /api/health 499 upstreamRqDuration 5006` and `4873` ms, "client has closed the request". Service metrics for 07:02:30 to 07:03:30: CPU 0.033 of a 2 vCPU limit, memory 0.20 of 1 GB, so nothing was starved; something waited.

Timeline: the new worker loaded its modules at 07:02:49.130 (its `startedAt`) and was healthy and switched in at 07:02:49.429 (`supervisor.since`). From then until 07:02:59.3 every request went to it and none was answered. One was answered at 07:02:59.39; the next took from about 07:03:00.4 to 07:03:02.6, ending when the log printed `Development sync v0.4.58: 2 created` at 07:03:02.670 and `weave supervise: image -> v0.4.58` at 07:03:02.676.

## Cause
After the old worker exits, the new worker settles its deferred open (`runDeferredMigrations`) and runs the boot syncs, synchronously, while it is already serving reads. `settleDeferred` forced a full rewrite of every workspace that had held any save (`#dirtyAll`: every entity row and its search entry), and `#migrate` forced the same for any repair at all. Locally, on a copy of the docs workspace (5,502 rows) and a 326-row workspace, the settle rewrote all 326 rows of workspace.db in 98 ms. Railway's volume is much slower, which fits the roughly ten-second block. The two-second block after it is the Development sync's own writes.

Local reproductions did not fail: macOS, and Linux node:22.23.2 with the data on a Docker volume, max latency 59 to 536 ms across a swap under a 50 ms health loop.

## What good looks like
A peer zero-downtime swap (nginx reload, systemd socket handoff) never blocks the serving process on bulk I/O: requests during a swap are answered within normal latency.

## Fix shape
1. Settle writes only what the deferred open changed: a held save keeps its dirty set, the structural rows are compared on every save, so a plain save is exact. `#migrate` marks every entity it changes (the v1 `doc` → `docs` pass was the one that did not) and stops forcing `#dirtyAll`. Gate: `test/supervisor.test.mjs` "settling a deferred open writes only what it changed, not every row" (41 rows written before, only the repaired table's registry row after).
2. If a stall remains on the next swap, move the boot syncs off the request path (yield between tables) and log step timings in the supervisor.

